**Amendment (Foundation public witnesses and integration layout)** · `amend:testing:foundation-layout` Refines (`dec:harness:levels`) for the existing foundation packages: tests/public.rs remains the external public-surface binary and tests/integration.rs remains the composition entry point. Focused sibling integration binaries are also declared integration suites in the checked registry: interchange keeps companion, deterministic_encoding, documents, inclusion, labels, prefix, properties, registry, restraint, satisfaction, theories, vectors, versions and benchmark_correctness; documents keeps panic_contract. Their tests/support modules and corpus files are support inputs, not executable suites. CLI common, execwrap and testkit use only the two standard external binaries. One focused public-surface witness suffices for each present package to distinguish external usability from internal access: interchange checks consumed prefix extent versus strict decoding; CLI common checks escaped control-record round-trip; execwrap checks empty-command refusal before effects; documents checks the public report schema; testkit checks complete named adapter results after failure. These witnesses do not claim exhaustive API coverage: the separately declared external composition suites supply the broader behavior and failure-boundary checks, and new public contracts must add their own evidence. No internal-module test is promoted to public-surface merely by its name.

**Amendment (Claims foundation evidence)** · `amend:testing:claims-foundation-evidence` The supported portion of (`gate:testing:foundation`), (`gate:design:claim-foundation`) has these premises: Current Rust and Python source carriers, the declared gate configuration and controlled graph fixtures; the reviewed glosses and area stakes are item-specific review evidence, not semantic facts proved by the graph evaluator. Its execution evidence is (`claim:testing:evidence-paragraphs-have-exact-edges`), (`claim:testing:every-rewritten-gloss-has-review`), (`claim:testing:evidence-exhibits-cannot-claim-support`), (`claim:testing:python-edges-require-known-claims`), (`claim:testing:sibling-failure-dominates`), (`claim:testing:execution-identity-required`), (`claim:testing:area-stakes-required`), (`claim:testing:source-obligations-cannot-disappear`), (`claim:testing:planned-names-are-not-evidence`), (`claim:testing:every-source-site-is-declared`), (`claim:testing:complete-executions-and-unique-mints`), (`claim:testing:cited-evidence-or-owned-holds`), (`claim:testing:requirement-edges-are-complete`), (`claim:testing:identifier-respellings-are-reported`), (`claim:testing:orphan-mints-are-refused`), (`claim:testing:respelling-switch-controls-exit`), (`claim:testing:review-is-bound-to-current-site`), (`claim:testing:source-carriers-are-lexically-checked`), (`claim:testing:exhibited-names-stay-out-of-census`). A supported row passes only when every cited claim and sibling passes for the checked source and configuration. This is scoped support, not full gate closure: tracing remains in step 4 and required service evidence remains held until provisioning; domain and client suites remain outside the current implemented surface.

**Amendment (Harness foundation evidence)** · `amend:testing:harness-foundation-evidence` The scoped support for (`gate:harness:foundation`) and (`gate:design:harness-foundation`) is recorded in the obligation data and the TESTKIT claim-area register at packages/testkit/README.md. Its evidence paragraph keeps package Rust claims as ordinary local citations and Python claims as qualified exhibits checked by ci/claims.py. Keeping implementation evidence in the package register preserves the citation-layer rule that repository-wide policy does not import package heads. These gates remain open only for tracing in step 4 and services.

**Amendment (Performance foundation evidence)** · `amend:testing:performance-foundation-evidence` The supported portion of (`gate:devperf:foundation`), (`gate:design:performance-foundation`) has these premises: The current gate profile and reviewed runner, structured current-source results and injected clock, cache, span, admission and duration fixtures; these establish reporter contracts and nonverdict behavior, not a performance baseline or controlled worker execution order. Its execution evidence is (`claim:devperf:correspondence-predicate-preserved`), (`claim:devperf:noisy-clock-is-nonverdict`), (`claim:devperf:cold-cache-is-nonverdict`), (`claim:devperf:overlap-is-not-summed`), (`claim:devperf:admission-is-nonverdict`), (`claim:devperf:category-controls-classification`), (`claim:devperf:exact-runner-version`), (`claim:devperf:timing-preserves-predicates`), (`claim:devperf:findings-preserve-outcome-order`), (`claim:devperf:missing-measurement-is-explicit`), (`claim:devperf:structural-errors-distinct`), (`claim:devperf:utc-is-explicit`), (`claim:devperf:runner-policy-is-nonverdict`), (`claim:devperf:result-accounting-is-explicit`), (`claim:devperf:raw-duration-validation`), (`claim:devperf:gate-profile-guards`), (`claim:harness:nextest-suite-arrival-envelope`), (`claim:harness:benchmark-regression-is-nonverdict`). A supported row passes only when every cited claim and sibling passes for the checked source and configuration. This is scoped support, not full gate closure: tracing remains in step 4 and required service evidence remains held until provisioning; domain and client suites remain outside the current implemented surface.

**Amendment (Carrier authority for evidence)** · `amend:testing:carrier-authority` Refines (`amend:testing:carrier-mapping`) and (`rule:testing:coverage`): the deployed linter proves placement and resolution for its Markdown and Rust carriers; ci/claims.py is the authority for Python docstring claims, their ordered carriers, unique mints, sites and source/configuration-matched results. Markdown names a Python-minted claim in double backticks, outside the linter citation graph; the obligation register carries the actual evidence edge with its premises and designated evidence paragraph. In those evidence paragraphs, every exhibited claim name must resolve to a Python mint in the checked census, and every Python edge must have its matching exhibit. Rust-minted claims retain ordinary linter-checked citations. Unknown edges, unknown or Rust evidence exhibits, and ordinary citations to Python claims refuse structurally. Planned names in other design paragraphs remain exhibits without becoming evidence or mints. The two carrier probes exercise these boundaries using controlled data. The six foundation rows support only their stated implemented portions; the gates themselves remain open only for tracing in step 4 and services.

**Amendment (Scoped support and review completeness)** · `amend:testing:scoped-support` Refines (`rule:testing:coverage`), (`proposal:testing:gate`) and (`amend:testing:carrier-authority`): the register uses scoped-supported and reports scoped_passed for passing evidence within the row's premises, never as a count of closed gates. Designated evidence paragraphs and the union of their rows' claim edges agree in both directions, including Rust citations and Python exhibits. The checked gloss baseline retains each current site's prior wording; every changed gloss requires its item-specific before/after review, and removing that review is structural. Source changes require an explicit baseline disposition rather than silently deleting a review obligation. Completed step 3b is no longer a closing step: storage history belongs to the durable-storage slice, client schema and packages to the client harness slice, release qualifications to their release boundary, and interchange law evidence to contract review before its first domain consumer. Policy obligations not supported by this foundation join remain owned by repository policy evidence admission before the first domain crate; observation coverage alone does not discharge their individual contracts. The measured decisions required by (`gate:devperf:foundation`) are recorded in (`amend:toolchain:measured-gate-profile`), (`amend:devperf:measured-choices`), (`amend:devperf:measured-runner`), (`amend:devperf:measured-license`), (`amend:devperf:measured-linker`), (`amend:devperf:measured-cache`) and (`reg:devperf:expensive`); reporter probes do not remeasure those campaigns.

**Amendment (Observation co-closure)** · `amend:testing:observation-co-closure` The current claim and gloss audit, bidirectional evidence edges, review roster and enforcement combine with (`amend:observe:foundation-evidence`) to support the implemented portions of (`gate:testing:foundation`) and (`gate:design:claim-foundation`). The register reports scoped support rather than unrestricted closed gates. Real PostgreSQL and Elements service evidence: service implementer, provisioning slice; domain suites: assigned domain implementer, corresponding domain bite; client harnesses and actual exporter transport admission: client implementer, client slice. Decision-level evidence is not implicitly closed by these scoped gate rows.

**Amendment (Python carrier adoption)** · `amend:testing:python-carrier-adoption` Supersedes the Python half of (`amend:testing:carrier-authority`) and (`amend:testing:carrier-mapping`). The deployed linter now reads tracked test_*.py docstrings under ci/ and tests/, using isolated Python syntax parsing without importing corpus code. ROOT activates both test and claim profiles for its Python sites; derived test labels use the integration area, while authored claim areas retain their own stakes. The eight identity-citation claim areas are respelled hashcitations because an area has no hyphen. Markdown references to Python claims are ordinary citations, including imported owner forms; exhibitions in evidence paragraphs are refused for either language. The repository join retains source-to-runner identity, generated Rust property-to-runner accounting, source/configuration results, sibling failure propagation, obligation and area edges, reviewed glosses and respelling enforcement. It no longer independently inventories Python commentary mints or validates Python carrier placement and derived labels. Its census must agree with the linter across both active languages. Planned design names remain exhibitions outside support.
